The 30 corrected symbols comprise ONE header-data symbol and TWENTY-NINE parity symbols. Data correction sets one captured-zero bit to one; parity correction sets36 bits to one. No one-to-zero correction occurs in this codeword. Corrected parity exactly matches re-encoding; the corrected codeword has zero syndromes under the same codec. A valid nearby codeword is not, by itself, independent proof of the original header contents when capture errors may exceed the correction bound.

The accepted header selects478 occupied 128-byte blocks. ALL478 remain uncorrectable, including the first. No recovered console.log is claimed. The selected data-parity bytes have per-bit one fractions0.38415,0.38942,0.39527,0.39229,0.39278,0.39438,0.39177,0.38298. There is no corresponding pre-reset parity snapshot, so these fractions alone do not measure bit-loss rate.

Dump header zones3,8,15,26,30 decode as empty DBGC records with correction counts23,26,27,21,18 respectively. Each changes one data symbol plus parity; all observed corrections set captured-zero bits to one. Other27 headers fail. Their sequence, followed by console30, agrees with the later live kernel's logged correction counts. That independently corroborates the on-device interpretation of the retained region; it does not locate when damage happened.

Conclusion and remaining gate
The earlier statement "30 corrected symbols" is a codeword-wide count, NOT30 header bytes or a recovered console. The actual capture reproduces the prior uncorrectable-data result and supports a directional clear-bit damage pattern relative to accepted nearby header codewords. It does not prove PMIC power collapse, DRAM retention failure, temperature effects, LK overwrite or extraction as the cause. Full power-loss recovery is not established. A matched pre-reset capture from the SAME ECC-configured run plus controlled reset/readback is still needed to identify the corruption boundary. That is future human-owned hardware work, not an overnight action.
